Feature: the resource sampler measures each role's agent process

  # BL-847 — DRAFT. Not a live .feature until the ticket is promoted and built
  # (BL-233: a live .feature with no step handlers hard-fails the runner).
  #
  # The pid resolution question in approval_context is unsettled, so the
  # scenarios below are written against the OBSERVABLE outcome (the recorded
  # sample describes the agent) rather than the resolution mechanism, and
  # should survive either ruling.

  Background:
    Given a swarm role is running an agent process inside its tmux pane
    And the pane's root shell is a different, much smaller process than the agent

  # BL-847 sample-describes-the-agent-01
  Scenario: a recorded sample describes the agent, not the pane's root shell
    When the resource sampler takes one sample for that role
    Then the recorded RSS for that role matches the agent process
    And the recorded RSS for that role does not match the pane's root shell

  # BL-847 no-agent-records-no-sample-02
  Scenario: a role whose agent cannot be identified records no sample at all
    Given no agent process can be identified inside that role's pane
    When the resource sampler takes one sample for that role
    Then no resource sample is recorded for that role

  # BL-847 shell-fallback-never-substitutes-03
  Scenario: the pane's root shell is never recorded as a fallback measurement
    Given no agent process can be identified inside that role's pane
    When the resource sampler takes one sample for that role
    Then the pane's root shell measurement is not recorded under that role

  # BL-847 respawn-detection-unaffected-04
  # getPanePid stays correct for PaneTailer: the pane's shell pid IS the right
  # identity for "was this pane respawned". Only the sampler's use changes.
  Scenario: pane respawn detection still identifies a pane by its own pid
    Given the pane has been respawned since the last observation
    When pane respawn detection runs for that role
    Then the respawn is detected from the pane's own pid
